WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
Pod and container security contexts define runtime privilege behavior.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:17.320
They influence the user identity inside the container, whether the workload may request more privileges, which Linux capabilities are available, whether privileged mode is allowed, and how filesystem restrictions apply.

3
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.600
.NET containers should normally run as non-root when the application supports it.

4
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:28.820
That design reduces default privileges and makes accidental writes or broad filesystem assumptions easier to detect.

5
00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:34.600
Running as non-root also forces the image and manifest to declare where the application is allowed to write.

6
00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:46.720
Settings such as runAsNonRoot, runAsUser, allowPrivilegeEscalation, capabilities, privileged mode, seccomp, AppArmor, and SELinux should be reviewed where platform policy supports them.

7
00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:53.380
The goal is to grant only the runtime behavior the application needs, not to preserve broad defaults because they are convenient.

8
00:00:53.660 --> 00:01:07.340
Read-only root filesystems can reduce accidental writes, but ASP.NET Core apps may still need planned writable locations for temporary files, uploads, logs, caches, or diagnostics.

9
00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:13.600
Those paths should be explicit, permissioned, and separated from application binaries.

10
00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:19.960
File ownership and permissions should be handled during image build or deployment rather than widened casually at runtime.

11
00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:30.620
Pod Security Standards, admission policies, and platform guardrails should align with application requirements so secure defaults do not become release surprises.
